    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1066">

                <pb facs="0940" n="933" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg933"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.5151 Manchuria/13: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-10-26T14:00:00+00:00">October 26, 1937—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 26—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>666. Substance of the Department’s 423, October 19, 3 p.m.,<anchor n="49"
                        xml:id="d1066-f1"/> was communicated informally to the Foreign Office on
                    October 20. The Foreign Office advises me today that instructions in
                    substantially similar terms to those of the Department were telegraphed on
                    October 22 to British representatives at Tokyo and Mukden.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1066-f1" n="49" xml:id="d1066-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported the telegraphic exchange October 18–19 with the Embassy
                    in China and instructed the Embassy in the United Kingdom to “take this matter
                    up with the Foreign Office”. (893.5151 Manchuria/11)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
